{"page":837,"blocks":[{"x":0.08775,"y":0.04911,"w":0.86376,"h":0.01851,"fs":0.01535,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"14.4 The Chain Rule 823"},{"x":0.09757,"y":0.08749,"w":0.24791,"h":0.07981,"fs":0.01151,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"To remember the Chain Rule, picture the diagram below. To find dw>dt, start at w and read down each route to t, multiplying derivatives along the way. Then add the products."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.09056,"w":0.59312,"h":0.04497,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"However, the meaning of the dependent variable w is different on each side of the preced- ing equation. On the left-hand side, it refers to the composite function w = ƒ(x(t), y(t)) as a function of the single variable t. On the right-hand side, it refers to the function"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.12442,"w":0.59308,"h":0.08784,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"w = ƒ(x, y) as a function of the two variables x and y. Moreover, the single derivatives dw>dt, dx>dt, and dy>dt are being evaluated at a point t0, whereas the partial derivatives 0w>0x and 0w>0y are being evaluated at the point (x0, y0), with x0 = x(t0) and y0 = y(t0). With that understanding, we will use both of these forms interchangeably throughout the text whenever no confusion will arise."},{"x":0.14451,"y":0.17051,"w":0.06363,"h":0.0118,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Chain Rule"},{"x":0.26033,"y":0.19243,"w":0.05669,"h":0.0237,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Dependent variable"},{"x":0.17373,"y":0.19558,"w":0.05866,"h":0.01164,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"w = f(x, y)"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.21286,"w":0.59302,"h":0.04544,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"The branch diagram in the margin provides a convenient way to remember the Chain Rule. The “true” independent variable in the composite function is t, whereas x and y are intermediate variables (controlled by t) and w is the dependent variable."},{"x":0.12601,"y":0.22155,"w":0.10148,"h":0.02417,"fs":0.01033,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"'y 'w"},{"x":0.21368,"y":0.22155,"w":0.01527,"h":0.01284,"fs":0.01033,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"'w"},{"x":0.